24. **Touch Gesture Disambiguation & Vertical Scroll Chaining Across Horizontal Media & App Rows**:
    - **Problem**:
      - On mobile touch screens, vertical scrolling worked smoothly over plain text and empty whitespace, but completely locked up when swiping up or down with a finger placed over horizontal app rows (`SquircleRowSection`, `ColumnStackSection`, `LandscapeCardsSection`, `EditorialFeatureCard`), video cards (`VideoCardsSection`), or screenshot galleries (`AppDetailPage`, `SearchResultsPage`).
      - While users could slide left and right horizontally, dragging vertically on any card, row, or video failed to scroll the page vertically.
    - **Root Causes**:
      - *Overscroll Containment Conflict*: `.scrollbar-hide` and `.overflow-x-auto` had `overscroll-behavior-y: contain` applied in `src/index.css`. Because horizontal rows have zero vertical scroll capacity, the browser immediately reached the Y-axis boundary and, per `contain`, strictly blocked the vertical scroll event from chaining (bubbling) to the parent `<main>` scroll container.
      - *Overflow Clipping*: The horizontal carousels possessed `overflow-y-hidden`, establishing a separate clipping context in the Y axis that suppressed vertical touch gestures on Chromium and WebKit.
      - *Rigid Scroll Snapping*: `snap-mandatory` forced mobile touch engines to lock exclusively into the horizontal snap axis, absorbing diagonal and vertical gestures.
      - *Wheel Dead Zone*: On desktop, `Layout.tsx` excluded `.overflow-x-auto` from mouse wheel event forwarding, preventing trackpad/mouse vertical scroll when hovering over app rows.
    - **Architectural Solution**:
      - *Vertical Chaining Guarantee*: Enforced `overscroll-behavior-y: auto !important;` on all `.overflow-x-auto` containers and removed `overscroll-behavior-y: contain` from `.scrollbar-hide`.
      - *Removed Y-Axis Clipping*: Eliminated `overflow-y-hidden` across all horizontal carousels, allowing native browser gesture disambiguation to distinguish between horizontal pans (< 45°) and vertical page pans.
      - *Fluid Snap-Proximity*: Converted `snap-x snap-mandatory` to `snap-x snap-proximity`, allowing smooth uninhibited gesture initiation without axis-locking.
      - *Universal 2D Touch-Action*: Configured `touch-action: pan-x pan-y` and explicit `overscrollBehaviorY: 'auto'` on all horizontal rows and media players.
      - *Desktop Wheel Forwarding*: Removed `.overflow-x-auto` from the `Layout.tsx` wheel event ignore-list so vertical wheel movements over horizontal rows seamlessly scroll the page.

25. **Independent Tab & Subtab Scroll Container Isolation Architecture**:
    - **Problem**:
      - Scrolling on the homepage affected other pages (such as the Profile / 'You' page). When switching to the 'You' page after scrolling deep into the homepage feed, the 'You' page appeared already scrolled to the bottom and would visibly jitter or auto-scroll back up.
    - **Root Causes**:
      - *Shared Mutable Scroll Parent*: The `<main>` element in `Layout.tsx` was configured with `overflow-y-auto` as the single shared scroll container for all tabs (`HomePage`, `ExplorePage`, `YouPage`).
      - *DOM Clamping on View Switch*: When switching tabs, `HomePage` was hidden (`display: none`) and `YouPage` was unhidden. Because the DOM height of `YouPage` was shorter than the deep scroll position of `HomePage` (e.g., 2000px vs 600px), the browser clamped the shared container's `scrollTop` to the maximum scrollable height of the newly mounted/unhidden `YouPage` (its bottom).
      - *Asynchronous Correction Loop*: Subsequent `scrollManager.restoreScroll` calls attempted to reset `scrollTop` from the clamped bottom position back to 0 across animation frames, creating a visible, disorienting auto-scroll jump from bottom to top.
    - **Architectural Solution**:
      - *Strict Scroll Container Isolation*: Converted `<main>` in `Layout.tsx` into a layout shell (`overflow-hidden`, non-scrollable) that forwards outer wheel and keyboard events to the currently active visible child.
      - *Dedicated Viewport Containers*: Each primary tab (`apps`, `search`, `you`) and `HomePage` subtab (`For you`, `Top charts`, `Categories`) now possesses its own dedicated DOM element with `overflow-y-auto`.
      - *Zero Cross-Contamination*: Because each view maintains its own isolated DOM scroll context, scrolling on `HomePage` has zero impact on `YouPage` or `ExplorePage`.
      - *Per-View Hook-Based Memory*: Hooked up `usePreserveScroll` directly to each dedicated container ref, ensuring each tab and subtab preserves its exact scroll offset independently without clamping, jumping, or background interference.

27. **Free-Sliding Carousels (Arbitrary Pause Position) & Robust Ancestor-Relative Section Scrolling Engine**:
    - **Problem**:
      - *Mandatory Carousel Snapping*: Horizontal store carousels (`VideoCardsSection`, `LandscapeCardsSection`, `ColumnStackSection`, `EditorialFeatureCard`) utilized CSS scroll snap rules (`snap-x`, `snap-proximity`, `snap-start`). When users swiped or dragged to browse apps, browser scroll snap engines forcefully intercepted deceleration and jumped/snapped cards to absolute item boundaries (`snap-start`), preventing users from pausing sliding smoothly at any custom or intermediate position.
      - *Section Scrolling Failure in Nested Viewports*: Calling `scrollIntoView()` on fields inside the publish wizard failed because `#root` and `body` enforce `overflow: hidden`, causing browser `scrollIntoView` implementations to target the outer non-scrollable document instead of the dedicated scrollable form viewport (`publishScrollRef` with `overflow-y-auto`). In addition, when validation triggered while the user was already on the same step (`targetStep === step`), React bailed out of state changes and never triggered the scroll effect.
    - **Architectural Solution**:
      - *Free-Sliding Carousel Engine*: Removed all `snap-x`, `snap-proximity`, `scroll-pl-1`, and `snap-start` rules across `VideoCardsSection`, `LandscapeCardsSection`, `ColumnStackSection`, and `EditorialFeatureCard`. Replaced with unrestricted, edge-to-edge touch/mouse momentum sliding (`touchAction: 'pan-x pan-y'`, `overscrollBehaviorX: 'contain'`), enabling users to swipe, slide, and pause smoothly at any arbitrary horizontal coordinate with zero forced snapping or absolute-position fitting.
      - *Ancestor-Relative Direct Container Scrolling*: Created `performScrollToTarget` which computes exact element positions relative to `publishScrollRef.current` (`elRect.top - containerRect.top + container.scrollTop - 32`) and directly commands `container.scrollTo({ top: desiredTop, behavior: 'smooth' })`.
      - *Same-Step Instant Execution & Multi-Frame Render Retries*: In `navigateToStepAndSection`, if `step === targetStep`, direct scrolling fires immediately via `requestAnimationFrame`. If the step must transition, an asynchronous retry loop checks across multiple animation frames (40ms, 90ms, 140ms...) ensuring the newly mounted DOM element is discovered, scrolled into view, autofocused, and highlighted with an active focus ring.
      - *Step 6 Direct Review Editing*: Embedded direct jump links across the parameters breakdown on Step 6, enabling one-click smooth traversal and autofocus directly into any individual section or field.

28. **Unrestricted Step Wizard Traversal with Preserved Error Handling & Feedback Loops**:
    - **Problem**:
      - Earlier navigation restrictions placed hard blockers in the step wizard tabs and the "Next" button, bouncing users back to earlier steps if any required fields (such as GitHub release URL, app name, binary download asset, or legal policies) were not yet entered.
      - This prevented publishers from freely browsing through future wizard steps, previewing the Review & Final Confirmation screen (Step 6), inspecting the metadata breakdown, or preparing screenshots and descriptions ahead of binary resolution.
    - **Architectural Solution**:
      - *Unrestricted Step Traversal*: Cleanly decoupled step navigation from field completion blockers. Publishers can freely click any of the 6 wizard tabs (`Release`, `App Info`, `Package`, `Description`, `Safety & Legal`, `Review`) and use the native `Next` and `Back` navigation controls without UI alterations or artificial gating buttons.
      - *Intact Error Handling & Validation*: Maintained 100% of validation logic, error handling, quota restrictions, and user feedback in `handlePublishApp` on Step 6.
      - *Targeted Guiding Feedback Loop*: When a publisher clicks "Publish App" on Step 6 with missing fields, the system activates the warning button state (`publishButtonState = 'warning'`), presents the descriptive toast alert (`Step X is incomplete: ...`), and triggers `navigateToStepAndSection`, instantly transporting the publisher back to the exact step, scrolling directly to the missing element, and applying an autofocus ring.

29. **Cloud Firestore Auto-Detect Transport & Multi-Tier Offline Resilience**:
    - **Problem**:
      - Hardcoding `experimentalForceLongPolling: true` during `initializeFirestore` forced the underlying WebChannel transport to strictly use HTTP long-polling while suppressing standard streaming protocols.
      - In sandboxed environments, Cloud Run ingress, or restricted iframe preview windows, HTTP long-polling connection handshakes that take longer than 10 seconds triggered a warning message (`Could not reach Cloud Firestore backend. Backend didn't respond within 10 seconds`).
    - **Architectural Solution**:
      - *Auto-Detect Long Polling*: Transitioned from `experimentalForceLongPolling: true` to `experimentalAutoDetectLongPolling: true`. The Firestore SDK now opportunistically leverages high-performance streaming first, seamlessly falling back to long-polling only if an intermediary proxy requires it, eliminating false-positive 10-second backend timeout warnings.
      - *Sandboxed Cache Fallback*: Wrapped `persistentLocalCache({ tabManager: persistentMultipleTabManager() })` with a dynamic fallback to `memoryLocalCache()`, ensuring compatibility in strict iframe environments where IndexedDB multi-tab locks may be constrained.
      - *Log Level Normalization*: Set Firestore log level to `'error'` (`setLogLevel('error')`) to filter out transient client-side connection status messages while preserving critical runtime exceptions.
      - *Multi-Tier Backend Fallback*: Enhanced `fetchAllAppsOnce` with a secondary fetch to `/api/apps` (server-side Firestore instance) and local cache so the store renders instantaneously regardless of client network latency.

30. **Super Administrator RBAC & Centralized Moderation Architecture**:
    - **Requirement & Problem**:
      - As the open marketplace expands with third-party app submissions and community reviews, the platform requires an authoritative governance layer to audit flagged apps, moderate abusive reviews, restrict malicious developer accounts, and permanently blacklist bad repository/release links without disrupting standard user browsing.
      - Administrative controls must be strictly gated to authorized administrator accounts (specifically designated root admin: `sahinurmolla190@gmail.com` and store administrator credentials) with zero permission leaks to regular users.
    - **Architectural Solution**:
      - *Super Admin Authorization Guard*: Implemented `isAdminEmail()` in `AuthContext.tsx` with dedicated authorization for `sahinurmolla190@gmail.com` and role-based permissions (`Store Administrator`, `Super Admin`). Derived `isAdmin` state across the application tree ensures privileged controls render exclusively for verified administrators.
      - *Contextual Publisher Header 3-Dots Menu*: In `PublisherConsolePage.tsx`, an administrative 3-dots menu button is injected into the publisher header exclusively when `isAdmin` is true. This provides one-click batch administrative actions:
        - Instant "Make All Publisher Apps Private" (hides all apps by a publisher from the public feed during investigation).
        - "Make All Publisher Apps Public" (restores app visibility after audit).
        - "Restrict Publisher" / "Remove Publishing Restrictions".
        - "Block & Suspend Publisher" (freezes publishing privileges).
        - "Open Admin Control Panel" shortcut.
      - *In-List App Moderation Popover*: Added contextual admin tools to each app's 3-dot dropdown in the publisher console, enabling single-item privacy toggles, instant app bans, permanent release link blacklisting, and administrative deletion.
      - *Dedicated Profile Admin Section*: In `YouPage.tsx`, an "Admin Control Panel" menu entry with live pending report count badges and an authoritative "Store Super Administrator" badge renders on the admin's profile.
      - *Admin Moderation Engine (`AdminModerationService`)*: Built a dual-layer local-first + Firestore synchronization service (`/src/services/adminModerationService.ts`) managing:
        - `AppReportItem`: User flag tracking with reason categories (malware, inappropriate content, broken download, copyright infringement), notes, and status workflows (`pending`, `reviewed`, `resolved`, `dismissed`).
        - `ReviewReportItem`: Abusive review flag moderation with one-click review deletion and dismissal.
        - `BannedReleaseLink`: Permanent SHA/URL blacklisting preventing re-upload of malicious GitHub release binaries.
        - `PublisherRestriction`: Granular publisher sanctions (posting limits, manual review requirements, account suspension).
        - `AppModerationState`: Real-time private/public and banned status enforcement integrated into `useStoreConfig` feeds.

